  // board: the blue of the RUA DO BAIRRO panel measures C* 16.1 and ours measured 13.3 — the
  // sky is the single largest region of the picture, so three points of chroma there is most
  // of the 16.3-against-19.5 the whole frame is short. Three waves have now walked into the
  // trap of paying for sky in the value channel and being refused by the print, so this one
  // does not touch value at all: the residual chroma is re-expanded AROUND the colour's own
  // Rec.709 luma, exactly the move waves 12 and 13 made on the hero and on the big canopy.
  // The luma the last two waves authored line by line comes out unchanged, and a sick sky —
  // which has almost no chroma to expand — stays bleached, because a gain on nearly zero is
  // still nearly zero.
  // ...and it is a DAYLIGHT gain only. Measured: run flat across the clock it takes the
  // night sky from C* 6.7 to 7.2 and the frame from 9.8 to 10.8, and that inverts the one
  // thing wave 12 bought — the hero stops being more saturated than his own street at NOITE
  // (ratio 1.00 -> 0.90) and his NOITE croma goes 12 -> 17 of 485. Held at zero after dark.
  const yy = 0.2126 * c[0] + 0.7152 * c[1] + 0.0722 * c[2];
