// ---------- NPCS — PESSOAS DA RUA ----------
// The board gives the street a cast, not a crowd: a wheelchair user, a woman in an apron, an
// older man in a hat, a kid, a grandmother, a man in a cap, and a dog. The game had none of
// them — it had one generic five-pixel figure in four arbitrary shirt colours, drawn in the
// pre-board palette. These are built the way the hero and the monsters are: a dark keyline
// all the way round, one light direction (the sun is up and to the right, so the right edge
// of every garment is the lit one), and every colour out of the board's own families.
//
// THEY ARE SCENERY. They carry no state, no hitbox and no entity: they are a function of the
// segment index and the world's health, exactly like a tree. Nothing here can be tapped,
// damaged, or collected, and none of it is reachable from the economy. That is also why they
// are drawn INSIDE the world pass, before the monsters and the drops — a neighbour must never
// be mistaken for something you are meant to hit, so they never stand in the road, never
// carry a health readout, and never wear the ticks that mean "this one is tappable".
// The neighbours are authored sprite frames now (npcA / npcB, three people per sheet) plus a
// walking dog, blitted HD over the world exactly like the hero. Still PURE SCENERY: a function
// of position, no state, no hitbox, not tappable, off the road — the block below only changes
// the picture. A gentle two-frame idle off tick (desynced per position); the dog gets its
// 4-frame walk. The procedural NPC_MAPS / NPC_C / npcSprite were retired with this pass.
